          ],
          "text": "Your child came into hospital at 6 hours of age with:\n- fast breathing\n- grunting\n- a temperature\n\nThe team found that your child had neonatal sepsis (a blood infection in a newborn). This was linked to a positive group B streptococcus (GBS) test in pregnancy and antibiotics given less than 4 hours before delivery."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "The team did a number of tests, including:\n- blood tests\n- a blood culture (to look for infection in the blood)\n\nThe blood culture showed no growth at 36 hours and was negative at 48 hours.\n\nYour child was given two antibiotics (benzylpenicillin and gentamicin) through a drip. The team decided to complete a 5-day course of these antibiotics based on the clinical picture and the blood test results. The course was completed on day 5.\n\nRoutine newborn checks were completed. Your child also passed the newborn hearing screen."
        },
